WEBVTT

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.850
The genius from the 
darkness, Akagi Shigeru,

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.520
versus Washizu Iwao, the 
monster of the Showa Era.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.560
Akagi bets his blood; 
Washizu wagers his entire fortune.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.090
The second match, South Hand 4.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.620
While Akagi leads by 9,200 points, 
the fateful west wind that enables

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.070
Washizu's haneman and an upset falls 
in the hands of his underling, Suzuki.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.870
900 CCs stand between Akagi and death.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.080
The footsteps of destruction 
draw closer and closer.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.200
<b>Episode 21</b>

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.200
<b>A Ray of Illusion</b>

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.450
<i>He'll die.</i>

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:05.830
<i>One misstep and that dora 
west wind will end it all.</i>

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.840
<i>Akagi will be killed.</i>

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.360
<i>Three of Washizu's tiles are black, 
which means that there is</i>

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.900
<i>no way he's got a double pon wait 
for the west wind and some other tile.</i>

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.850
That's because only one of the four 
of a particular tile is black.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.850
<i>At least that risk is zero.</i>

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:26.590
<i>But...</i>

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.590
<i>With this setup, he could have 
Suzuki discard the west wind.</i>

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.200
Pon.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.930
<i>Then switch to a naked wait and 
have Suzuki deal into that, too.</i>

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.070
<i>He'd have the green dragon, west wind 
honitsu with three dora. A haneman.</i>

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.710
<i>He'll take the lead.</i>

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.210
<i>Or...</i>

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:56.180
Were he to be in a chanta tenpai...

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.300
he could still have Suzuki 
deal him the dora west wind

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.720
and then deal him the 9 of 
characters for his single wait.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.760
That would be a green dragon, west wind 
chanta with three dora, a haneman.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.060
It would cause an upset.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.220
<i>He'll die.</i>

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.440
<i>If that's the state of the 
enemy's hand, it's over.</i>

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.080
<i>Akagi will be killed and I'll lose an arm.</i>

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.670
<i>We'll lose all the money, too.</i>

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.080
<i>We'll be bankrupt. On the retreat.</i>

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.590
<i>If the dora west wind comes out now...</i>

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.240
<i>If it comes out now...</i>

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.300
<i>We're saved.</i>

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.830
<i>The enemy's hand isn't quite there.</i>

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.200
<i>It goes without saying 
that he's in tenpai.</i>

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.740
<i>But he can't win right away, 
even if he calls the west wind.</i>

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.330
<i>Which means we still have a chance.</i>

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.350
<i>If he gets into tenpai now...</i>

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.350
<i>If he...</i>

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.200
But Akagi's draw is useless.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.930
<i>Damn, a north wind at a 
critical time like this!</i>

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.890
<i>What a lousy draw.</i>

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.200
<i>I'm begging you!</i>

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.790
<i>We can't waste a single second here!</i>

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:54.800
<i>No!</i>

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.850
<i>Why didn't you discard 
the west wind, the dora?</i>

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.360
<i>It's the shackle that's been 
binding you this whole time!</i>

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.290
<i>So isn't this precisely 
the time to get rid of it?</i>

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:07.310
<i>Since the enemy didn't call it this turn?</i>

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:14.320
<i>If you wait a turn, 
assuming that Washizu didn't have</i>

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.880
<i>a honitsu already, 
but looked like this instead...</i>

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:19.880
If he draws the 7 of bamboo,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.320
he can switch to a double pon wait 
for the west wind and 7 of bamboo.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:29.000
<i>Once that happens, he won't be able 
to discard the west wind anymore.</i>

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.010
<i>Were he to discard it,</i>

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.590
<i>it would be a mangan direct hit with the 
green dragon, west wind, three dora.</i>

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.960
<i>He'd lose his lead.</i>

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.840
<i>There's no way he could discard it then.</i>

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.920
<i>If you think of that,</i>

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:47.200
<i>wasn't this your only opportunity 
to get rid of that damned west wind?</i>

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.850
<i>So why didn't you?</i>

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:52.590
<i>Wait.</i>

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.330
<i>Is it possible that Akagi thinks</i>

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:59.090
<i>Washizu is waiting to win 
on a single west wind wait?</i>

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:02.330
<i>Even if he's almost certain 
that Washizu has a pair,</i>

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.330
<i>he may think there's a slight chance 
that he's got a single wait.</i>

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.490
<i>Is that why he couldn't discard</i>

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.550
<i>this dora west wind?</i>

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.580
<i>There's a 9,200 point gap between them.</i>

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.160
<i>Washizu can't make up that 
difference from a tile dealt by Suzuki.</i>

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.930
<i>But were Akagi to deal into his hand,</i>

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.990
<i>it would be a green dragon 
honitsu with two dora, a mangan.</i>

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.850
<i>Does he think Washizu could be 
waiting on a single west wind?</i>

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.610
<i>Is that the illusion...</i>

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.490
<i>the fear...</i>

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:39.910
<i>that has him trembling?</i>

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.690
<i>He got it.</i>

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.820
<i>Now he can get in tenpai and have 
a closed wait on the 2 of characters.</i>

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.790
<i>He can get into tenpai!</i>

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:58.380
<i>But to do that, he has to... 
he has to discard the west wind!</i>

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.160
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.100
If Washizu has a single 
wait on a west wind,

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.310
he'll have a green dragon honitsu 
with two dora, a mangan.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.590
He would overturn Akagi's lead.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.670
<i>This tile is the angel of death itself.</i>

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.890
<i>A tile that leads to death.</i>

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:19.640
<i>He can't discard it.</i>

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:21.500
But if Washizu

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.160
already has two west winds and is 
waiting for something else entirely,

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.650
keeping the west wind in his hand will 
cause a fatal stall in Akagi's game.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.980
Something he can't do.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:40.990
<i>Washizu's tenpai is a sure thing and 
Suzuki can give him the dora at any time.</i>

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.670
<i>That's a haneman with the green dragon, 
west wind honitsu and three dora.</i>

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.900
<i>He can gift Washizu 12,000 points easily.</i>

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.230
<i>Which means this last hand is 
not the time to fool around.</i>

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:56.920
<i>If he doesn't get into tenpai, it'll be fatal.</i>

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.980
<i>He'll lose even that 
faint chance he has now.</i>

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.610
<i>So which is it?</i>

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.850
<i>Do you see it as a single 
wait, or as a pair?</i>

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:38.050
I thought you'd try to make it pass here.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:42.480
If it came down to that.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:46.630
<i>It passed!</i>

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.440
<i>Akagi's instinct won out.</i>

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.720
<i>He finally made it to tenpai 
by discarding that dora.</i>

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.300
Akagi has an iipeikou with a closed 
wait for the 2 of characters.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.690
<i>Now we just need Yasuoka to get 
a 2 of characters and we'll win.</i>

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.750
Of course, Washizu is currently in tenpai.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.540
It just happened to be that

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.300
the tile he was waiting for 
was a 2 of characters.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.430
The same wait as Akagi's.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.990
Washizu is in a closed wait for a 2 
of characters, the same wait as Akagi.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.320
Since the wait is for the 
same 2 of characters,

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.040
it might seem like their chances 
of winning are the same,

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.930
but that couldn't be 
further from the truth.

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:05.110
Akagi can only win with 
this 2 of characters,

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:11.460
but Washizu can also use the dora, 
the west wind, to change his hand.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:14.690
If you consider that his underling 
can deal into his hand right away,

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:16.940
the number of Washizu's winning 
tiles increases greatly.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.450
For instance, if he draws the 1 of 
characters and changes his wait to this

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:28.990
double pon wait, he can win by Suzuki 
dealing the west wind into his hand.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:34.190
Alternatively...

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:36.780
If Suzuki draws that 1 of characters,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.460
he can just call the west wind and then 
have Suzuki deal that into his hand.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:47.290
That would be enough for 
the haneman upset with

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.230
the green dragon, west wind 
chanta, with three dora.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:54.880
On top of that, if you stick with the 
situation where Washizu calls the west wind,

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.010
then if Washizu draws any honor or circle tiles

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:05.710
near the ones in Suzuki's hand, 
he can win from those, too.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:11.460
Basically, once he draws one, 
he can keep it in his hand

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.090
and discard a character tile.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.700
Then he just has to call Suzuki's west wind

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.650
and have Suzuki deal into his single wait.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:27.540
That will become the haneman 
hand that will overtake Akagi.

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:32.760
Under the premise that 
he calls the west wind,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.550
Washizu has a total of nine 
different tiles he can wait on.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:39.710
On the other hand, 
Akagi only has one.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.290
Only this closed 2 of characters.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:45.600
A huge gap!

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:48.300
Akagi is at a huge disadvantage.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:52.280
Akagi knows that better than anybody else.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.730
And then he gets a chance 
to switch up his hand.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:00.950
<i>What will you do?</i>

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.830
Will he stick with his closed 
wait for the 2 of characters?

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.300
Or discard the 3 of characters to 
get a 1, 4, 7 of characters wait?

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.950
Or discard the 1 of characters 
to get a 3, 6 of characters wait?

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:17.610
<i>I guess the safest bet would be 
to discard the 3 of characters</i>

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:20.460
<i>and go for the more favorable 
1, 4, 7 of characters wait.</i>

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.120
<i>In any case, he should just 
stay in tenpai for now.</i>

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.900
<i>He can declare riichi after Yasuoka 
has drawn the winning tile.</i>

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.660
<i>Declaring riichi without 
having the goal in sight</i>

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:35.570
<i>would just be doing Washizu a favor.</i>

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:38.120
If he loses the riichi wager,

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:41.410
Akagi          41900 points

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:41.410
Difference 9200 points

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:41.410
Washizu 32700 points

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.100
the difference between them

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:42.510
would shrink by 2,000 to just 7,200 points.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:50.610
Difference 7200 points

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:50.610
Washizu   33700 points

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:50.610
Akagi      40900 points

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:46.000
That will remove the necessity for Washizu

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:48.300
to get 12,000 points to take the lead,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.590
as the 8,000 from a mangan would suffice.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:55.090
<i>He can't do that. 
That'll just hurt him too much.</i>

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.250
<i>So he has to be careful with the riichi.</i>

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:02.790
<i>Of course, waiting to declare 
riichi until Yasuoka has the winning tile</i>

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:07.750
<i>will delay his win by one turn, 
and that itself may be fatal, too.</i>

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:11.860
<i>But still, a riichi here can't happen.</i>

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.240
<i>It's unthinkable!</i>

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.990
But Akagi...

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.150
contradicts Ougi's expectations completely,

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:39.970
and declares it!

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:46.080
Riichi.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:53.090
And it's a 3, 6 of characters wait.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:14:00.890
<i>He went for the wait with the most 
unlikely tiles, and he declared riichi?!</i>

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.720
<i>Why would he do that now?!</i>

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.290
<i>Could it be... a premonition?</i>

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.890
<i>Did he have a premonition 
that Yasuoka will</i>

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.840
<i>draw his winning tile 
in the next turn?</i>

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:25.190
<i>If that's the case, I suppose 
I could understand it.</i>

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:28.080
<i>But is something like 
that really possible?</i>

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:36.090
<i>Akagi... Are you really telling 
me that I'm going to draw it now?</i>

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.590
<i>Is that what you're feeling right now?</i>

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.070
<i>Is your instinct telling you</i>

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:47.070
<i>that I'll draw it?</i>

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:52.610
<i>Did he get it?!</i>

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:53.810
<i>Did he?!</i>

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:20.730
<i>What?</i>

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:23.130
<i>Since he declared riichi,</i>

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.820
<i>I thought the cop would deal 
straight into his hand.</i>

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.240
<i>But that is not the case.</i>

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:32.480
<i>You've dug your own grave.</i>

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:38.730
<i>You buckled under the pressure of 
the match and fell in by yourself.</i>

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.160
<i>I'm sure you must know what it means.</i>

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:49.500
<i>The meaning of the 9,200 
point difference between us.</i>

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:55.400
<i>Until now, I needed a mangan tsumo, 
or a haneman if I win off Suzuki,</i>

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.530
<i>in order to take the lead.</i>

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:02.010
<i>But once you declare riichi, 
everything changes.</i>

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:05.560
<i>Now a tsumo on a 5,200 
hand or a mangan hand</i>

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.010
<i>from a tile dealt by 
Suzuki will be enough.</i>

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.990
<i>I can take your lead.</i>

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.950
<i>You should have never called 
a riichi unless you were</i>

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.530
<i>absolutely certain your partner 
could deal into your hand.</i>

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:28.410
<i>Did you crack from the pressure?</i>

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:31.540
<i>I can't say I don't understand that.</i>

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:35.980
<i>My advantage over you was just 
too much, too overwhelming.</i>

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:39.950
<i>Maybe you thought I would end 
the match in the next turn.</i>

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:45.920
<i>You thought that and decided 
to make it all or nothing.</i>

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:51.560
<i>Making the cop your last ray of 
hope, you declared your riichi.</i>

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:56.060
<i>You figured that if you 
have to die anyway,</i>

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:58.410
<i>you'd die while running towards victory.</i>

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:06.490
<i>Like a prisoner about to make a break,</i>

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:12.080
<i>knowing he has practically no chance 
to escape but trying it anyway...</i>

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.510
<i>You wanted to believe in miracles?</i>

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:27.820
<i>Reality is overwhelmingly cruel.</i>

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:34.330
<i>It's that tiny ray of hope 
that seals your fate.</i>

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:40.850
<i>While I had the advantage, 
only your riichi finally managed</i>

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.080
<i>to break the balance of power.</i>

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.640
<i>The final door has opened.</i>

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:49.540
<i>The angel of death has crept in!</i>

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.390
Pon!

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.740
And now, I win!

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:13.810
<i>Oh, no!</i>

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.230
<i>He'll play into his hand and it'll be over!</i>

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.770
A mangan with the green dragon, 
west wind, and three dora.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:22.310
<i>He'll die.</i>

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:28.880
<i>He'll die!</i>

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.230
Ron!

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:49.640
I win!

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:51.720
And now you'll die!

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.140
And still, you don't tremble?

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:02.980
But now you'll have your blood drawn 
from you and you will most certainly die.

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:08.010
I wonder how long you can remain calm.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.370
Th-The 3 of characters?

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:25.520
Too bad.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:28.610
It's a head bump.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.520
<i>Wh-What?</i>

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:46.350
You were getting all worried, 
so you tried to force an ending.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.530
If you hadn't done that, 
you would have won.

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:51.220
If you had restrained yourself,

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:52.860
you would have won in 
another three or four turns.

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:55.980
What are you scared of?

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:04.610
You're actually quite the 
coward, Washizu Iwao.

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:08.110
<i>This is impossible!</i>

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.850
<i>A 3, 6 of characters wait?</i>

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:13.850
<i>And you declared your riichi by 
discarding the 1 of characters?!</i>

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:16.410
Akagi could have stayed with the

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:18.540
closed wait for the 2 of 
characters for an iipeikou.

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:20.450
Or if he had to declare riichi, 
he could have done it

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.040
with a 1, 4, 7 of characters wait 
for more possibilities to win.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:27.270
And yet he deliberately chose 
the 3, 6 of characters wait.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:31.570
<i>He manipulated me?</i>

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:36.970
<i>He saw through my hand 
and manipulated me?!</i>

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:44.480
<i>This bastard!</i>

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:49.170
The second match ends, 
again with Akagi as the victor.

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:53.290
But Washizu is the man once 
known as the king of the shadows.

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.690
There is no way he will back down now,

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:57.710
from this battle where both

00:20:57.710 --> 00:20:59.960
Akagi and Washizu have 
wagered their own demise.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:05.100
The night of darkness 
gets deeper... and darker.

